\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The universal pattern.
\f0\b0 \'a0The design principle that applies to any agentic system\cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Claude Code\'92s manifestation.
\f0\b0 \'a0One specific, production-grade implementation of that pattern\cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0
\f1\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Your translation.
\f0\b0 \'a0How this looks in your system, which may not be a code agent at all\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 \strokec2 A terminal-first code agent expresses these primitives through slash commands, shell safety layers, and remote bridges. A customer-support agent, workflow orchestrator, or AI-powered SaaS feature will implement the same primitive very differently.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa385\partightenfactor0

